# Backstop — live chain wiring
#
# The ERC-8183 hire flow (src/lib/chain/hireAgent.ts) is wired against the
# real @bnbagent/sdk package and the contracts it already has deployed on
# BSC Testnet (chain id 97) — no contract deployment needed on your side.
#
# Without these set, the app falls back to the simulated hire stepper that
# was already in place — nothing breaks, it just doesn't touch the chain.

# The hirer's wallet. Get testnet BNB from the BSC Testnet Faucet (linked
# from the hackathon's technical resources) so this wallet can pay gas, and
# fund it with the ERC-8183 payment token ("United Stables" / U) the same
# way. First run imports PRIVATE_KEY into an encrypted local keystore
# (~/.bnbagent/wallets) under WALLET_PASSWORD; you can omit PRIVATE_KEY on
# later runs once the keystore exists.
PRIVATE_KEY=
WALLET_PASSWORD=

# Optional: a provider address to use for agents that don't have their own
# providerAddress set yet (see src/lib/agents.ts). This lets you smoke-test
# that createJob -> registerJob -> fund actually lands on BSC Testnet before
# you have a real agent deployed and listening. Any valid 0x address works;
# the job just won't ever get fulfilled unless something is really listening
# for it via ERC8183JobOps / fundedJobWatcher.
DEMO_PROVIDER_ADDRESS=

# Once you've deployed a real agent via the BNB Agent Studio CLI
# (npm install -g @bnbagent/studio-cli, then `bag skills install`), put its
# real on-chain address on that agent's `providerAddress` field in
# src/lib/agents.ts instead of relying on DEMO_PROVIDER_ADDRESS.

# Wallet connect (Reown AppKit — src/lib/wallet/config.ts). Get a free
# project id at https://dashboard.reown.com (Reown Cloud → Create Project
# → AppKit). Without this, the header's "Connect wallet" button renders
# disabled rather than crashing — nothing else in the app depends on it yet.
NEXT_PUBLIC_REOWN_PROJECT_ID=

# --- Altana assurance-pool vault (Altana partner track) ---
# The pool is an Altana smart wallet. Its ADMIN key can grant/revoke a
# scoped SESSION whose only power is transfer() on the payment token, up
# to a spend cap — that session is what actually pays a rebate. Two
# separate keys on purpose: the admin key never touches the live payout
# path, so a leaked session key is bounded by its own cap and expiry.
#
# Setup (one time): fund a fresh throwaway wallet with a little testnet
# BNB, put its key below, then run:
#   npx tsx scripts/provision-altana-pool.ts
# This grants + registers the session in the Altana Keystore (~$0.50 fee,
# paid from the admin wallet) and prints an ALTANA_SESSION value to set
# below. Without ALTANA_SESSION set, src/lib/wallet/altanaPool.ts reports
# `mode: "unconfigured"` rather than touching the chain.
#
# ALTANA_SESSION is base64, not raw JSON — paste the script's output
# exactly as printed. Next.js's own .env loader applies shell-style $VAR
# interpolation to every .env* value, which silently corrupts the raw
# session JSON (it always contains a literal "$bigint" key); base64 has no
# `$` in its alphabet, so it survives untouched. Never hand-edit this value.
ALTANA_ADMIN_PRIVATE_KEY=
ALTANA_SESSION=

# Daily rebate spend cap in payment-token base units (default 500 U —
# 5e20 at 18 decimals). Only read by the provisioning script.
ALTANA_POOL_DAILY_CAP=500000000000000000000
